{{#if (or (ne elixirDeploy "none") (eq elixirClustering "libcluster"))}}
## Releases

Use Elixir 1.17 or newer. The Docker builder pins Elixir 1.20.4 and OTP 29.0.6.

```sh
MIX_ENV=prod mix deps.get --only prod
MIX_ENV=prod mix release
_build/prod/rel/{{elixirAppName}}/bin/{{elixirAppName}} start
```

Release settings are read at startup through `config/runtime.exs` and `rel/env.sh.eex`.
Keep `mix.lock` with the project and review dependency updates before rebuilding a release.
The generated Dockerfile can be built with `docker build -t {{elixirAppName}} .`.
{{#if (ne elixirWebFramework "none")}}
Set `SECRET_KEY_BASE` and `PHX_HOST` in the deployment environment before starting the release.
{{/if}}
{{#if (eq elixirClustering "libcluster")}}

### Clustering

Set `RELEASE_COOKIE` to the same private value on every node. Set a unique, reachable
`RELEASE_NODE` such as `{{elixirAppName}}_a@10.0.0.10` on each node. The release enables long node
names; it refuses to start without a cookie. Keep Erlang distribution on a private network.

For Docker, Gigalixir, and standalone Mix releases, set `CLUSTER_HOSTS` to comma-separated full
node names. The EPMD strategy reconnects every second. Alternatively, set `DNS_CLUSTER_QUERY`
to a DNS name whose addresses correspond to `{{elixirAppName}}@<address>` nodes. Explicit hosts
take precedence over DNS. Empty discovery settings leave the node unclustered.
{{#if (eq elixirDeploy "fly")}}

Fly uses the application's internal DNS and `FLY_PRIVATE_IP` automatically. Set the same
`RELEASE_COOKIE` secret on its machines. Its release uses IPv6 Erlang distribution.
{{/if}}

Gigalixir must supply a unique `RELEASE_NODE` and the shared cookie in its release environment.
Choose discovery values that resolve inside that application's private network. These settings
do not provision machines or certify a hosted deployment.
{{/if}}

{{/if}}
